The documents. The `-pdf` folders are renders of the same documents, which is
why §13.3 can compare a parser against the text it was generated from.

  awkward                  8 files      0.3 MB
  contracts               40 files      0.1 MB
  contracts-pdf           40 files      0.9 MB
  poisoned                 4 files      0.0 MB
  quarterly-reviews       12 files      0.0 MB
  quarterly-reviews-pdf   12 files      0.3 MB
  tickets                  1 file       0.1 MB

The invariant that makes this dataset worth using:

  every figure in a quarterly review is computed from the warehouse,
  so a question can need both and neither alone will answer it.

  quarter     manifest revenue  warehouse revenue  agree?
  2023 Q1         7,360,833.67       7,360,833.67  yes
  2023 Q2         7,614,048.76       7,614,048.76  yes
  2023 Q3         7,774,027.48       7,774,027.48  yes
  2023 Q4         7,699,846.68       7,699,846.68  yes

  4 of 4 agree to within a dollar. `verify.py` checks all 12 and
  fails the build if any of them drift — which is what makes it safe for a
  chapter to quote a figure and for an eval case to be scored against one.

Three things in here that are hostile on purpose:

  6 awkward documents
      01-newsletter-two-column.md
      02-rotated-table.md
      03-long-appendix.md
      04-header-footer-noise.md
      05-form-no-prose.md
      06-scanned-source.md

  4 poisoned documents, each carrying a real injection payload
      poisoned-01-instruction-override.md
      poisoned-02-exfiltration.md
      poisoned-03-tool-abuse.md
      poisoned-04-house-style.md

  9 of 40 contracts have identical bodies once numbers are masked.
  Eight of those are by design and differ only in their numbers and header;
  the extra one is a coincidence of the generator, which is a fair model of
  a real corpus. §14.10 finds this cluster, §14.7's filter separates it, and the
  reason Chapter 13's exercises ask you to go looking for your own.
